<https://concepts.datalad.org/s/demo-research-information/ns/entity-roles/1160471a-9f8e-4379-85f2-351a2a36ef40> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/annotations> _:rbd4f51c8a2c06c33d73bda6c_nf42b8a0050434306b6f084715a5f8611b2 .
<https://concepts.datalad.org/s/demo-research-information/ns/entity-roles/1160471a-9f8e-4379-85f2-351a2a36ef40> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/name> "Long-term goal" .
<https://concepts.datalad.org/s/demo-research-information/ns/entity-roles/f47b8f5e-8781-4f3b-89d9-8d1fb1f815e9> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "A Drittmittelanzeige is (in German academic/administrative contexts) a notification or declaration that a researcher or university employee files to report third-party funding (Drittmittel) received (or applied for) for research or projects. It typically serves these purposes:\n\n- Documents the source, amount, and purpose of external funds (e.g., grants from industry, foundations, EU, government agencies).\n- Ensures compliance with institutional rules on acceptance and use of third-party funds (conflict-of-interest checks, approval by the employer/institution).\n- Triggers administrative handling (accounting, budgeting, cost-center assignment, overhead/indirect cost processing).\n- Records any obligations or restrictions attached to the funding (reporting, IP/ownership, publication embargoes).\n\nFormat and procedures vary by institution: some use an online form, others a written request to the department or research office. If you want, I can draft a sample Drittmittelanzeige form or checklist tailored to a university." .
