_:r36305852a1a1b22696688898_nae2babf3f2e9427b97580136c8a756e0b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/DOI> .
_:r36305852a1a1b22696688898_nae2babf3f2e9427b97580136c8a756e0b1 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2020.116860" .
_:r36305852a1a1b22696688898_nae2babf3f2e9427b97580136c8a756e0b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/abstract> .
_:r36305852a1a1b22696688898_nae2babf3f2e9427b97580136c8a756e0b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/AttributeSpecification> .
_:r36305852a1a1b22696688898_nae2babf3f2e9427b97580136c8a756e0b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#value> "AbstractAs evidenced by the present special issue, movie fMRI is emerging as a powerful tool for exploring brain function and characterizing its variation across individuals. Here, we provide a brief perspective on the potential of movie fMRI for advancing the discovery of brain imaging-based markers of psychiatric illness. We discuss relevant gaps and opportunities in movie fMRI, and propose community-level models that might accelerate the pace of discovery of fMRI-based biomarkers in psychiatry." .
_:r36305852a1a1b22696688898_nae2babf3f2e9427b97580136c8a756e0b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> .
_:r36305852a1a1b22696688898_nae2babf3f2e9427b97580136c8a756e0b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/AttributeSpecification> .
