_:rd9827c1ea10b62253e364e7b_n206eea81ff9e463aa1bb64bc2f5e7b53b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/AttributeSpecification> .
_:rd9827c1ea10b62253e364e7b_n206eea81ff9e463aa1bb64bc2f5e7b53b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#value> "Probabilistic cytoarchitectonic map of Area 33 (ACC) (v16.1)" .
_:rd9827c1ea10b62253e364e7b_n206eea81ff9e463aa1bb64bc2f5e7b53b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/abstract> .
_:rd9827c1ea10b62253e364e7b_n206eea81ff9e463aa1bb64bc2f5e7b53b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/AttributeSpecification> .
_:rd9827c1ea10b62253e364e7b_n206eea81ff9e463aa1bb64bc2f5e7b53b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#value> "This dataset contains the distinct architectonic Area 33 (ACC) in the individual, single subject template of the MNI Colin 27 as well as the MNI ICBM 152 2009c nonlinear asymmetric reference space. As part of the Julich-Brain cytoarchitectonic atlas, the area was identified using cytoarchitectonic analysis on cell-body-stained histological sections of 10 human postmortem brains obtained from the body donor program of the University of Düsseldorf. The results of the cytoarchitectonic analysis were then mapped to both reference spaces, where each voxel was assigned the probability to belong to Area 33 (ACC). The probability map of Area 33 (ACC) are provided in the NifTi format for each brain reference space and hemisphere. The Julich-Brain atlas relies on a modular, flexible and adaptive framework containing workflows to create the probabilistic brain maps for these structures. Note that methodological improvements and integration of new brain structures may lead to small deviations in earlier released datasets. Other available data versions of Area 33 (ACC): Palomero-Gallagher et al. (2018) [Data set, v16.0] [DOI: 10.25493/S6PK-Z7E](https://doi.org/10.25493%2FS6PK-Z7E) The most probable delineation of Area 33 (ACC) derived from the calculation of a maximum probability map of all currently released Julich-Brain brain structures can be found here: Amunts et al. (2019) [Data set, v1.18] [DOI: 10.25493/8EGG-ZAR](https://doi.org/10.25493/8EGG-ZAR) Amunts et al. (2020) [Data set, v2.2] [DOI: 10.25493/TAKY-64D](https://doi.org/10.25493/TAKY-64D)" .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/DOI> .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb1 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "10.1038/s41467-022-29766-8" .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#object> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/demo-research-information/ns/persons/simon-eickhoff> .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/Attribution> .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/AttributeSpecification> .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#value> "Shared and unique brain network features predict cognitive, personality, and mental health scores in the ABCD study" .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/abstract> .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/AttributeSpecification> .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#value> "How individual differences in brain network organization track behavioral variability is a fundamental question in systems neuroscience. Recent work suggests that resting-state and task-state functional connectivity can predict specific traits at the individual level. However, most studies focus on single behavioral traits, thus not capturing broader relationships across behaviors. In a large sample of 1858 typically developing children from the Adolescent Brain Cognitive Development (ABCD) study, we show that predictive network features are distinct across the domains of cognitive performance, personality scores and mental health assessments. On the other hand, traits within each behavioral domain are predicted by similar network features. Predictive network features and models generalize to other behavioral measures within the same behavioral domain. Although tasks are known to modulate the functional connectome, predictive network features are similar between resting and task states. Overall, our findings reveal shared brain network features that account for individual variation within broad domains of behavior in childhood." .
_:rd983d4cf2611ea454ca7d544_n4cebd88dd0e648099d3f806a4fa909aeb5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#object> <http://purl.org/ontology/bibo/AcademicArticle> .
